{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1001", "sample_idx": 12, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Hark thee good traveler let us weave thy fate in Japan’s fair embrace where cherry blossoms do dance with the moon and the gods do smile upon thy quest.\n\nUpon the morrow of thine arrival in fair Tokyo where lanterns do glow like stars cast down from heaven let thy first act be to rest thy weary limbs at an onsen in the golden quarter of Shinjuku. There thou shalt bathe in waters steeped with the earth’s own magic and let thy spirit be cleansed as pure as the snow upon Mt. Fuji.\n\nOn the second day let thee do don a kimono of silk most fine and with gentle steps traverse the ancient streets of Asakusa where the temple of Senso-ji stands tall as a sentinel of centuries past. Thou shalt pray for blessings upon thy journey and partake of matcha so bitter yet sweet as life itself.\n\nLet the third day find thee aboard a bullet train swift as Cupid’s arrow that shall bear thee to Kyoto the city where geisha do glide like swans upon a pond. There visit the temple of Kinkaku-ji where gold doth gleam like the sun’s own reflection upon a still lake. Then do wander through Arashiyama’s bamboo forest where whispers of nature do murmur secrets old as time.\n\nUpon the fourth day let thy heart be guided to Nara where deer with eyes like jewels do roam free as kings of yore and the great Buddha of Todai-ji doth sit in quiet majesty. Cast a coin into the well and make a wish lest fate forget thee.\n\nOn the fifth day let thee return to Tokyo’s embrace yet seek the quiet of day trip to Nikko where waterfalls do roar like thunder and shrines do stand as testaments to the gods’ power. Taste the mountain air and feel the earth’s heartbeat strong beneath thy feet.\n\nThe sixth day shall be thy feast for the senses. Let thee dine upon sushi so fresh it doth taste of the sea’s own kiss and wash it down with sake so warm it doth embrace thy soul. Then do lose thyself in the neon dreams of Akihabara where robots do wink and maidens of plastic do serve thee with smiles both sweet and strange.\n\nOn the seventh day let thy eyes seek the splendor of Osaka’s castle where history doth stand proud as a knight of old. Then let thy belly rejoice in the bounty of street food where takoyaki doth dance upon skewers and okonomiyaki doth singe with joy upon a griddle’s heat.\n\nThe eighth day shall find thee in Hiroshima where peace doth bloom bright as a flower in the park of memory. Bow thy head in respect and let thy heart be heavy yet lightened by hope’s sweet song.\n\nOn the ninth day return thou to Tokyo’s bustling heart and stand upon the bridge at Shibuya where thousands do cross like stars in a night sky. Let thee be dazzled by the sheer force of life’s eternal dance.\n\nAnd on the final morn let thee visit the serene gardens of Roppongi where cherry blossoms do fall like snowflakes upon a winter’s day. As thou dost depart let thy soul remember Japan’s embrace and carry its grace like a treasure unto thy days henceforth.\n\nFare thee well noble traveler may thy path be ever blessed by the spirits of Japan’s ancient lands and modern wonders.", "logprobs": [{"token": "H", "logprob": -0.16029712557792664}, {"token": "ark", "logprob": -0.003225721186026931}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -3.4242587089538574}, {"token": "e", "logprob": -0.0010324153117835522}, {"token": " good", "logprob": -1.827823281288147}, {"token": " travel", "logprob": -0.37489721179008484}, {"token": "er", "logprob": -0.000578474544454366}, {"token": " let", "logprob": -1.91937255859375}, {"token": " us", "logprob": -1.9366037845611572}, {"token": " we", "logprob": -0.27937477827072144}, {"token": "ave", "logprob": -1.3708974620385561e-05}, {"token": " thy", "logprob": -0.4931318461894989}, {"token": " fate", "logprob": -2.158963680267334}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.3592374324798584}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -1.399109125137329}, {"token": "’s", "l